WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.263
JENNIFER THOMAS: Have you
heard the phrase, change

00:00:02.263 --> 00:00:03.960
is the only constant in life?

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.940
While that may be
accurate for most things,

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.860
you don't want it to be true
for your infrastructure.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.520
Let's take a look at how Critter
Junction learned the hard way

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.637
about adopting immutable
infrastructure.

00:00:14.637 --> 00:00:21.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.320
Mutable means capable of change.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.070
In this case, we're
talking about the idea

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.330
of cloud resources that are made
to never have manual changes.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.830
For our friends at
Critter Junction,

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.450
they started with
virtual machines

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.520
that hosted their web servers.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.290
Their VMs were
originally designed

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.630
to be mutable because of
the short-term flexibility

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:40.740
it gave them.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.150
Each VM could more precisely
fit the apps that ran on it,

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.520
and it was easy to run custom
updates for each machine.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.790
The team often became attached
to individual machines, which

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.190
was occasionally useful
if they needed to fix

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.510
specific problems quickly.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.810
For example, they
ran a VM with NGINX

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.220
in version 1 of
their web server,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:00.870
with data written locally.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.611
When they wanted to switch to
Apache, it was easy for them

00:01:03.611 --> 00:01:05.069
to perform an
upgrade on the server

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:07.740
directly and run version 2.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.080
On the surface,
this seems great.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.667
They were using
an existing server

00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.875
and didn't have to worry
about moving the data around

00:01:13.875 --> 00:01:17.140
to other machines or
creating a new machine.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.860
But in the real world,
things can go wrong.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.590
The Apache upgrade could have
failed for a number of reasons,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.210
like networking issues.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:25.710
In their case,
some of the servers

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.310
updated quickly, while others
got hung up on installations.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.790
They were left with some servers
running version 2 successfully,

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:35.280
some that upgraded the
app but couldn't run it,

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.340
and some servers that were
still running version 1.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.500
Many of their VMs were failing
in slightly different ways.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.010
And they were now
facing complexity

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.350
with user traffic being
routed to different running

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.870
versions of their web
server, an inability

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.090
to roll back to a
previous version,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.340
and undocumented
update statuses,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.430
making it impossible
to track versions.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.990
Instead of changing each
of their existing VMs,

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.730
they should have changed their
environment to be immutable.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:06.090
Once a VM is deployed,
it can't be modified.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.810
No updates, no patches,
no configuration changes.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.970
If you want to change
application code

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.360
or apply a patch, you instead
would build a new image

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.850
and deploy it as a replacement.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.770
Because new environments
can be spawned

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.860
in the cloud in a
matter of minutes,

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:26.160
immutable infrastructure is
easy to architect and deploy.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.330
After some long hours restoring
version 1 of their application,

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.930
here's how Critter Junction
handled their next deployment.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.540
Instead of making changes
in place to each machine,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.420
they created a brand
new VM image with Apache

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.720
and version 2 of their app, and
moved any locally written data

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.060
to an attached persistent disk.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.850
After some testing to make
sure the new image was working,

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.800
they spun up multiple
servers using the new image.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.120
Now they could safely switch
traffic to their new VMs

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.540
and shut down the instances
running version 1.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.570
On Google Cloud, it looked
like this for their team.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.750
They used Cloud Build to set
up continuous integration

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:07.620
with their repository,
automating billing and testing.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.410
VMs were specified
using image families

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.770
so they could have multiple
versions of their images

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.910
and deprecate the
newest versions if they

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.440
needed to roll back.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.140
And they specified all
of their resources using

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.000
Cloud Deployment
Manager in order

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.790
to automatically provision it.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.520
This is the same pattern as
working with containers--

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.230
create a complete
runtime environment

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.910
and deliver that as an image.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.770
With containers, the
runtime environment

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.530
is a program and the
files it needs to run.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:36.480
With immutable
infrastructure, you're

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.570
delivering a whole operating
system, configured as needed,

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:43.290
along with the programs
and their files.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:47.310
In both cases, you continuously
deliver versioned images.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.110
Creating immutable
infrastructure

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.500
gave Critter Junction protection
from configuration drift,

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.140
since they now know the
exact state of their machines

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.050
and can avoid any
unexpected surprises.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:01.530
In addition, they can now
easily track versions, roll back

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:05.220
releases, and have a more
consistent testing process,

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:06.810
thanks to the
documented differences

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:08.830
between their environments.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.340
Immutable infrastructure
might sound at odds

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.110
with agile architecture.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:15.420
But in reality, it
gives you agility

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.940
because you know the
specifics and code

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.600
status for every resource
in your environment.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.780
Stay tuned to find out what's
in store for Critter Junction.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.690
And remember-- always
be architecting.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:31.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

